WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.370
Post-quantum cryptography is not only a research topic.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.990
It is a practical migration issue for any organization that relies on public-key

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.615
cryptography for key exchange,

00:00:08.615 --> 00:00:12.820
digital signatures, certificates, software updates, service authentication,

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.545
or long-lived confidentiality.

00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:18.760
Quantum-capable attacks are not something most security teams can observe directly today,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.741
but migration work has to start before systems are already under pressure.

00:00:21.741 --> 00:00:25.900
NIST standards matter because they give vendors, governments, architects, developers,

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.815
auditors, and security teams a shared reference point.

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:29.650
Without standards,

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.785
every organization would have to interpret the algorithm landscape on its own.

00:00:33.785 --> 00:00:37.455
Standards reduce uncertainty by naming the algorithms, parameter sets, interfaces,

00:00:37.455 --> 00:00:41.570
and expected security categories that can be used as the basis for products and migration

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:42.280
planning.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.530
It helps to separate several stages that are often blended together.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.425
Algorithm selection means NIST has chosen an algorithm family for standardization.

00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:53.225
Standardization means the formal standard has been published.

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:55.808
Implementation means libraries and products actually support it.

00:00:55.808 --> 00:00:59.380
Validation means an implementation can be tested through recognized programs.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.560
Deployment means real systems have adopted it safely, interoperably, and at scale.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.240
Security practitioners do not need to become cryptographers to use the standards well.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.480
They do need to understand what each standard is for, where it may appear in real systems,

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.235
and what migration questions it raises.

00:01:13.235 --> 00:01:16.895
The practical job is to connect standards to inventory, vendor readiness,

00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:19.880
protocol choices, testing, key management, and staged rollout.
